## CQL Query Engine

Run CQL (Chess Query Language) queries against a PGN corpus. New engine
service alongside stockfish/maia3, plus a gateway /query route and a
chess-lms /api/cql proxy.

## Requirements
- user supplies a PGN file per request (their own games, lesson games)
- user supplies free-form CQL; the engine returns matching game positions
- synchronous, seconds-scale latency (small-to-mid corpora, capped sizes)
- later: school database of all student games (admins/coaches upload);
  the corpus is a parameter from day one so that phase is additive only

## Spike results (CQL 6.2, build 9.493 — verified against the real binary)
- the reference implementation is gady costeff's CQL:
  https://www.gadycosteff.com/cql/ — prebuilt for win/mac, and a source
  zip for linux (cql6-source-9-493.zip). it is a STANDALONE executable:
  no gcc, no per-query codegen (the compile-cache plan is dropped).
  alternative: CQLi by robert gamble (cql64.com), a faster CQL 6.1
  interpreter — candidate for a later swap, same http contract.
- linux build (per the zip's readme, verified on trixie gcc 14): extract
  to cqlsourcedist/, uncomment the gcc OSFLAGS + LINUXPTHREADOPTION lines
  in makefile-flags, `make cql`. ~65 MB with -g; strip brings it down.
- invocation: `cql -s -noheader -i in.pgn -o out.pgn -matchstring
  cqlmatch query.cql`. command-line options OVERRIDE the cql file header,
  so the caller controls input/output even when the query sets `input`.
  -s for single-threaded (robust on hand-made pgn). -noheader drops the
  per-game "{Game number N cqlmatch}" comment, which contains the
  matchstring and would masquerade as a ply-0 match.
- output contract (verified): each matched position is marked by a
  {cqlmatch} comment AFTER the san that produced it; a match at the
  start position is a bare {cqlmatch} before the first san. a game that
  matches but has no matching positions cannot occur (game matches iff a
  position matches). zero matches = exit 0 + empty out.pgn.
  fens come from replaying the parsed moves.
- security-relevant filters exist (readfile/writefile/persistent, and
  `input` in the cql header): run in a fresh temp dir per request as cwd,
  non-root container, no network egress. cli -i/-o pin the corpus i/o.

## Decisions
- implementation: wrap reference CQL 6.2 (see spike). no compile cache,
  no gcc in the runtime image.
- sync request/response (no jobs) — scan is seconds-scale for the capped
  corpus sizes; revisit async only for the school database.
- no response cache in v1: user corpora are one-off queries. Revisit if
  the school database creates hot repeated queries.

## Data Model

No MODELS entry — cql is not a predict model (no elo/checkpoint/cache
semantics). The gateway routes /query to a single engine url.

env:
  CQL_ENGINE_URL : internal cql engine api url (gateway side)
  CQL_PATH       : path to the cql executable (engine side, default "cql")

## API Design

### Gateway: POST /query
Auth: same X-API-Key as /predict. Not cached.

{
  query       : string   // cql source, capped (4 KB)
  pgn         : string   // inline corpus, capped (5 MB)
  max_matches ?: int     // capped at 100, default 25
}
-> {
  matches     : { game: int, ply: int, fen?: string, move?: string,
                  white?: string, black?: string, date?: string }[]
  total_matches : int
  truncated   : bool     // total_matches > len(matches)
  scanned_games : int
}

- game is the 0-based index into the input pgn; ply is the half-move
  number of the matched position (0 = before the first move); move is
  the san leading into the position ("" for the start position); fen is
  the position itself, omitted if replay failed
- gateway enforces size caps only (query length, pgn bytes, max_matches)
  via http.MaxBytesReader; chess-content validation happens in the engine
- return match locations, not PGN blobs — small responses, cache-safe
- callEngine passes r.Context() (http.NewRequestWithContext); /query is
  the longest request and client disconnects should propagate

### Engine: POST /query
Same request/response shapes as the gateway route.

Per-request flow:
1. validate: query non-empty + under cap; pgn under cap and under the
   game cap (light structural count — cql parses the games itself)
2. run: fresh temp dir; write query.cql + input.pgn; execute
   `cql -s -i input.pgn -o out.pgn -matchstring cqlmatch query.cql`
   with a 120s context (hard kill on expiry; nothing persistent to
   keep warm); stdout/stderr captured for diagnostics
3. parse out.pgn: split games (header blocks + movetext), scan movetext
   for marker comments while counting san tokens (ply), replay each
   matched game with notnil/chess up to the last matched ply for fens
4. respond; delete the request's temp dir

Missing out.pgn or a nonzero exit without output = 500 with a stderr
snippet. An exit code with a produced out.pgn is parsed regardless —
cql's exit convention for "no matches" is not load-bearing.

Latency budget: seconds-scale for the capped corpus (no compile step).
Engine handler timeout 120s.

### chess-lms: /api/cql proxy
Mirror /api/engine (app/api/engine/route.ts):
- Supabase session required (not an open relay)
- caps: query 4 KB, pgn 5 MB, max_matches 100, 60s AbortSignal.timeout
- MOVE_GATEWAY_URL + MOVE_GATEWAY_API_KEY server-side only
- maps gateway errors to friendly messages (query syntax, size caps,
  timeout) so the query editor can point at the problem

## Sandbox posture (free-form CQL)
cql is a query interpreter, but its filters can read/write files:
- dedicated container: non-root, no network egress, concurrency 1
- fresh temp dir per request (cwd), removed after; no other writable path
- cli -i/-o pin corpus i/o; container fs is otherwise read-only to the
  process user; Cloud Run CPU/memory limits bound resource use
- response capped by max_matches (a query matching every position would
  otherwise emit a huge output pgn — parsed but truncated in response)

## Limits (summary)
  query        4 KB      enforced gateway + engine
  pgn          5 MB      enforced gateway + engine
  games        20000     engine, structural count
  max_matches  100       enforced gateway + engine
  scan timeout 120s      engine, hard kill
  lms timeout  60s       /api/cql AbortSignal

## Work breakdown
1. spike: done (see above) — contract pinned, source zip build verified
   on first docker build
2. engines/cql service: main.go (server + runner), parse.go (output pgn
   parsing + replay), Dockerfile (cql built from pinned source zip),
   compose entry (:8083), testdata/fake-cql.sh test double
3. gateway POST /query: size caps, MaxBytesReader, context-aware
   callEngine
4. tests: valid/invalid output parsing, oversized query/pgn, game cap,
   timeout kill, truncation, gateway caps + proxying
5. chess-lms /api/cql proxy + env wiring (reuses MOVE_GATEWAY_URL)
6. deploy: separate Cloud Run service for the cql engine, wire
   CQL_ENGINE_URL

## Later phases
- school database: corpus becomes corpus_url (Supabase signed URL) or
  corpus_id — the engine contract already treats the PGN as a parameter.
  Signed URLs keep Supabase credentials out of cloud-move.
- shared-corpus caching: key = hash(query) + hash(corpus) once hot
  repeated queries exist
- async jobs (submit + poll) if school-database scans exceed the
  sync budget
- curated query library in the LMS (pins, hanging pieces, ...) reusing
  the same endpoint — free-form ships first, curated is product work
